# past.dev vs mem0

Source: https://past.dev/vs/mem0

mem0 is an open-source memory layer that extracts memories from conversations and stores them in a vector store you configure, scoped per user, session or agent. past.dev is a memory API that accepts timestamped text and returns ranked documents with dated source excerpts. It runs as a managed service or, on an Enterprise agreement, in your own cloud.

## What mem0 is

[mem0](https://github.com/mem0ai/mem0) is an Apache 2.0 memory layer for AI agents, with Python and TypeScript SDKs. It can run three ways: as a library inside your process, as a self-hosted server, or as their managed platform. The core surface is small, `add()` writes memories from messages and `search()` retrieves them, and memory is scoped at user, session and agent level.

A language model extracts memories as short statements about a user. A configured vector store keeps those statements for retrieval by user, session or agent scope.

## What past.dev is

past.dev runs as a managed service or, on an Enterprise agreement, in your own cloud. You `POST` raw text with its original timestamp, wait until ingestion completes, and query it later. `/recall` returns ranked documents with occurrence dates and source excerpts for the requested identity.

Stable source ids support replacement, ingestion ids support deletion, and an optional recall timestamp supplies a historical query perspective.

## Architecture differences

|  | mem0 | past.dev |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Licence | Apache 2.0 | Commercial |
| How you run it | Library, self-hosted server, or managed platform | Managed API, or your own cloud on an Enterprise agreement |
| What you send | Conversation messages | Any text plus the timestamp it happened, backdatable |
| Unit sent | Conversation messages | Timestamped source text |
| Storage | A vector store you configure | Managed by past.dev, or your own database on an Enterprise agreement |
| Scoping | user, session and agent identifiers | Project, audience slug, and identity |
| A source changed | The memory is updated | Reuse its stable source id for the new revision |
| Reading | Search returns memories | `/recall` returns ranked documents |
| Says it does not know | Your code decides | Your code evaluates returned documents |
| Cost visibility | Your model provider's bill | Accounted per call on the console dashboard |

## How to choose

Choose based on the requirements below.

- **Choose mem0** if you want to own the whole memory in your own infrastructure, if your input is chat and your questions are about a user's stable preferences, or if an Apache 2.0 licence is a requirement.
- **Choose past.dev** if you ingest timestamped emails, transcripts, tickets, and notes, or if recall must return dated source excerpts under identity and audience scoping.
- **Neither is a framework.** Both leave your agent, your model and your prompts where they are.

