## Frequently asked questions

### What is the best memory layer for a legal AI assistant?

A layer that separates clients, follows the firm's ethical walls, stores the date of every document, returns sources with every fact, and keeps earlier values when terms change. past.dev is a memory API built for that pattern. A temporal knowledge graph such as Zep's Graphiti fits when the assistant must traverse legal relationships. Consumer chat memory does not fit client files.

### How is AI used in law firms?

Firms use AI to draft, review documents, research, summarize matters, and answer questions about the firm's own files. That last use needs memory: a store of the firm's documents with dates, sources, and access rules per matter.

### How do we build ethical walls into an AI assistant?

Keep the walls in the firm's walls system and sync them into the memory layer. With past.dev, each matter becomes an audience that lists the lawyers allowed on it, every matter document names that audience, and each client has its own project. The application must pass the signed-in lawyer's identity on every recall.

### How do we sync Intapp Walls or iManage walls into past.dev?

Run a sync job in your integration code. When the walls system opens or changes a wall, write the matter's allowed lawyers to an audience with one API call, then apply the change with the recompute call. Without the recompute call, the change still reaches recall within seconds.

### Can memory carry across matters without leaking through ethical walls?

Yes, when the memory layer enforces walls on derived memory as well as on documents. In past.dev, a memory is never readable by more people than the documents it came from, so a summary built from a walled matter stays behind that wall.

### Can a legal AI answer what was true on a past date?

Yes, when the memory keeps document dates and earlier values. With past.dev, send the recall with queryTimestamp set to that date, and only evidence that occurred at or before it is eligible.

### Does using AI waive attorney-client privilege?

That is a legal question for the firm's counsel. In United States v. Heppner (S.D.N.Y., 2026), a federal court held that documents created with a public-facing AI platform were not privileged. A memory layer controls where client files are stored and who can read them. With the firm's own database or cloud account, stored files stay in that account.

### Can we keep client data on our own infrastructure?

Yes, on an Enterprise agreement. past.dev can store memory in a database that you own, or run inside your AWS, Google Cloud or Azure account, operated by us, with the same routes as the managed service.

### Will past.dev train on our matters?

No. past.dev states that it does not use customer data to train models, and that model providers process requests with zero retention. Confirm the terms in the data processing agreement before production.

### Is past.dev an alternative to Harvey or Legora?

No. Harvey and Legora are legal AI platforms that lawyers use directly. past.dev is the memory layer a firm or a legal AI company builds under its own assistant, including the permissions and ethical walls that memory across matters requires.

### Does past.dev replace Westlaw or Lexis?

No. It remembers the firm's own documents and any text you send, with dates. Checking legal authority still needs a citator.

### Can we delete a document and the facts extracted from it?

Yes. Delete it by the document id you sent, or delete the whole send by its ingestion id, and the memory derived from it is erased. Project-level and account-level erasure are available on request.

### Is this legal advice?

No. past.dev stores and returns dated evidence. The firm remains responsible for professional obligations, conflicts decisions, and the advice it gives clients.

